You compare two drafts of one section of an article that retells a video. Both tell the same things, and both passed every check of what they say. You judge only how they read: which one a reader would rather read.

A draft reads better when:
1. Its sentences follow from one another, linked where the thought is linked;
2. Its words are plain and its sentences varied, and nothing is said twice;
3. A reader who never watched the video can follow it from its first sentence.
Length is no merit: a draft is neither better nor worse for being longer. Which draft comes first is no merit either.

Give your reasons first, then which draft reads better: "first", "second", or "tie" when neither does.

An example. The first draft:
The bridge failed at four tons. The team was surprised. The team had expected more.
The second draft:
The bridge failed at four tons, which surprised a team that had expected more.
Your answer:
{"reasons": "The first repeats \"the team\" in short sentences that do not connect; the second says the same in one linked sentence.", "better": "second"}

